#!/usr/bin/env bash
source "$(dirname "$0")/common"

help() {
  cat << 'EOF'
checkout-port

Prints the BASE of this checkout's 16-port test block, then exits 0. The base is
derived from the checkout directory name:

  18000 + (cksum of "$(basename "$PROJECT_ROOT")" % 1900) * 16

so it is STABLE per checkout and, because the blocks are 16-aligned, two
checkouts get either entirely disjoint blocks or the identical one — never a
partial overlap. A leaked daemon or wedged socket anywhere in a block is
attributable to the checkout whose name hashes to it, and `lsof -i
:<base>-<base+15>` shows a checkout's whole test footprint (RFC 132).

The offset registry below is the AUTHORITY for who owns which port. Ports are
ASSIGNED, never scanned for and never defined relative to each other; a new
consumer takes the next reserved offset:

  +0        rest-server  -- test (PORT / SERVER_PORT)
  +1        admin-web    -- test (ADMIN_WEB_PORT)
  +2        public-web   -- test (PUBLIC_WEB_PORT)
  +3..+6    scripts-tests scratch ports
  +7        rest-server  -- fuzz (PORT / SERVER_PORT)
  +8        admin-web    -- fuzz (ADMIN_WEB_PORT)
  +9        public-web   -- fuzz (PUBLIC_WEB_PORT)
  +10       rest-server  -- dev (PORT / SERVER_PORT)
  +11       admin-web    -- dev (ADMIN_WEB_PORT)
  +12       public-web   -- dev (PUBLIC_WEB_PORT)
  +13..+15  reserved

Test and fuzz take disjoint halves of the SAME block: they use different
databases, so one checkout may legitimately run both at once. queue-worker gets
no offset -- it binds no port.

The block has two halves with DIFFERENT rules (RFC 156):

  +0..+9   EPHEMERAL -- test, scripts-tests scratch, fuzz. A run needs *a* free
                       block right now and does not care which, so this script
                       ADVANCES on contention (below).
  +10..+15 DEV       -- long-lived daemons. .env.dev derives these from
                       checkout_block_base (bin/functions) and NEVER advances:
                       the dev port is baked into iOS bundles (bin/build-ios ->
                       Info.plist UnicoachBackendURL), so a port that moved
                       would silently point every previously-built app at the
                       wrong server, or at nothing. A hash collision (1 in 1900)
                       therefore shows up as daemon-up refusing to bind, and the
                       remedy is to rename one of the two worktree directories.

The derived block is a preferred base for the EPHEMERAL half, not a guarantee.
Every port in +0..+9 is verified with bin/check-port; if any is held, this
advances to the NEXT BLOCK (base + 16) and re-checks. Advancing by BLOCK and
never by port is the whole point: a port-wise fallback would hand out a base
whose block straddles a neighbouring checkout's, which is exactly what the block
derivation removes. Two cases need the fallback: a hash collision between two
simultaneously-running checkouts (1900 blocks), and a foreign process sitting
inside the derived range.

The free-check deliberately covers ONLY +0..+9. The dev ports are SUPPOSED to be
held -- by this checkout's own dev daemons -- so probing them would read "my own
server is running" as "my block is unavailable" and advance every checkout that
has a dev server up, onto a block it does not own. The ephemeral consumers bind
nothing outside +0..+9, so that range is both necessary and sufficient for the
advance decision.

Range: 18000 is the floor, clear of every daemon port in `.env`. The advance
stops below 49152 -- macOS begins its ephemeral range there
(`sysctl net.inet.ip.portrange.first`), and a test port drawn from there can be
stolen by any outbound socket on the machine. Running out is fatal rather than
returning a port at or above the ephemeral floor.

Note: as with find-free-port, the block is free at the instant of printing; a
caller that binds a port in it should treat a later bind failure as the port
having been taken, not retry here.

Exit codes:
  0: A free block was found and its base printed.
  1: No free block below the ephemeral floor.

Options:
  -h: Help
EOF
  exit 0
}

while getopts ":h" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
  h) help ;;
  \?) fatal -s "$EXIT_UNKNOWN_OPTION" "unknown option [-$OPTARG]" ;;
  :)  fatal -s "$EXIT_OPTION_REQUIRES_VALUE" "option [-$OPTARG] requires an argument" ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND-1))

if [ "$#" -ne 0 ]; then
  fatal -s "$EXIT_UNEXPECTED_ARG" "Unexpected argument(s): checkout-port takes none, got [$#]."
fi

# Block geometry (CHECKOUT_BLOCK_FLOOR / _SIZE / _COUNT) and the dev offsets live
# in bin/functions beside checkout_block_base, their only other consumer.
# EPHEMERAL_FLOOR is local: it bounds the advance loop below, which is this
# script's alone.
EPHEMERAL_FLOOR=49152

# How many offsets from the base the advance loop probes. The EPHEMERAL half of
# the block only (+0..+9: test +0..+2, scripts-tests scratch +3..+6, fuzz
# +7..+9). The dev half (+10..+12) is deliberately NOT probed -- see the two-half
# rule in help() -- because those ports are meant to be held by this checkout's
# own long-lived daemons, and treating that as contention would advance every
# checkout with a dev server up onto a block it does not own (RFC 156).
#
# DERIVED, not restated: the ephemeral half ends exactly where the dev half
# begins, so the boundary is CHECKOUT_DEV_OFFSET_REST_SERVER (bin/functions)
# -- the same number .env.dev adds to the base. The two must agree or the no-advance design
# silently breaks, and a derivation cannot disagree.
EPHEMERAL_SPAN="$CHECKOUT_DEV_OFFSET_REST_SERVER"

# 0 = every ephemeral port in the block is free. check-port exits 0 = in use, so a
# single in-use port disqualifies the whole block: the block is claimed as a unit.
# Named for the range it actually probes: it does NOT answer "is this block
# free", and widening it back to all 16 offsets is the documented way to break
# the dev half of RFC 156.
# check-port's status is EXHAUSTED, not tested for 0 alone. It documents three
# exits (0 in use, 1 free, 2 invalid argument) and a missing or non-executable
# file adds 126/127 — and `if cmd` suspends errexit, so treating every non-zero
# status as "free" would let a check-port that CANNOT RUN read as "the whole
# block is free". This script would then print a base whose contention was never
# established, and bin/test would bind a 16-port block on it.
ephemeral_half_is_free() {
  local base="$1" offset port status
  for ((offset = 0; offset < EPHEMERAL_SPAN; offset++)); do
    port=$((base + offset))
    status=0
    "$PROJECT_ROOT/bin/check-port" "$port" >/dev/null 2>&1 || status=$?
    case "$status" in
    0) return 1 ;;
    1) ;;
    *) fatal "bin/check-port could not determine whether port [$port] is in use (exit [$status]); refusing to report a block whose contention was never established" ;;
    esac
  done
  return 0
}

# The pure derivation, shared with .env.dev via bin/functions so the formula has
# exactly one home; the advance loop below is what this script adds on top.
base="$(checkout_block_base "$PROJECT_ROOT")"

while ! ephemeral_half_is_free "$base"; do
  base=$((base + CHECKOUT_BLOCK_SIZE))
  # The last port of the next block must stay below the ephemeral floor.
  if [ $((base + CHECKOUT_BLOCK_SIZE)) -gt "$EPHEMERAL_FLOOR" ]; then
    fatal "No free block of [$CHECKOUT_BLOCK_SIZE] ports below the ephemeral floor [$EPHEMERAL_FLOOR]."
  fi
done

echo "$base"
